[{"_1":2,"_3":4},"root",{"_5":101},"routes/item",{"_5":6},"data",{"_7":8,"_9":10},"item",{"_11":12,"_13":14,"_15":16,"_17":18,"_19":-5,"_20":21,"_22":23,"_24":25,"_26":27,"_28":27,"_29":30,"_31":32,"_33":34,"_35":36,"_37":38,"_39":40,"_41":-5,"_42":43,"_44":45,"_46":47,"_48":49,"_50":51,"_52":53,"_54":36,"_55":56,"_57":58,"_59":36,"_60":61},"expiresAt",1793621624000,"id","bnzk9rpw17dy0icteiecasgmr","title","攻击者借伪造 Adobe Acrobat 页面分发 ScreenConnect 建立远程访问","originalTitle","Hackers Pushing Remote Access Tool Through Fake Adobe Acrobat Pages","summary","CloudSEK 发现攻击者利用伪造 Adobe Acrobat 与文档签名页面分发合法签名的 ConnectWise ScreenConnect 客户端，通过预配置连接至未获授权的租户，使外部操作者可能持续访问 Windows 系统。","reason","source",{"_72":100},"links",{"_61":99},"publishedAt","2026-11-02T11:17:11.000Z","discoveredAt","2026-11-02T11:46:34.792Z","timelineAt","category","activity","tags",[75,73,96,97,98],"score",69,"selected",false,"channel","news","story",{"_77":78,"_13":79},"x","readingMode","full","author","Deeba Ahmed","body",{"_92":-5,"_61":93,"_94":-5,"_95":36},"outline",[80,81,82],"relatedStories",[76],"topics",[68,69],"indexable","markdownAvailable",true,"group",{"_62":63,"_64":65,"_66":67},"hasTranslation","bodyLanguage","original","factId","f5l4S-BiIL5M","reportCount",1,"additionalSourceCount",0,{"_70":74,"_72":75},{"_70":71,"_72":73},"slug","malware","name","恶意软件","attack","攻击活动",{"_77":78,"_13":79},"publicId","8706e80e-4992-445f-916d-08a3f1b79613","CloudSEK披露仿冒页面投递ScreenConnect",{"_11":90,"_84":91,"_86":87},{"_11":88,"_84":89,"_86":87},{"_11":83,"_84":85,"_86":87},"sec-3","text","Advice for Security Teams","level",3,"sec-2","Configuration and Persistent Access","sec-1","Fake Pages and Screening Logic","zh","<p>Cybersecurity researchers from CloudSEK have uncovered a malicious campaign that uses fake Adobe Acrobat and document-signing web pages to distribute legitimate <a href=\"https://hackread.com/connectwise-screenconnect-tops-abused-rats-2025/\">ConnectWise ScreenConnect</a> remote-access software. </p><p>In their research shared with Hackread.com, researchers found that attackers were abusing digitally signed installers configured to connect to unauthorized remote-access tenants, potentially giving external operators persistent access to Windows systems.</p><p>This is not the first time criminals have abused ScreenConnect. Hackread.com has previously reported on similar campaigns, including attacks using <a href=\"https://hackread.com/hackers-screenconnect-hijack-pcs-fake-social-security-emails/\">fake Social Security Administration emails</a> and <a href=\"https://hackread.com/fake-word-phishing-enterprise-blind-spot-trusted-remote-access-tools/\">Word Online pages</a> to distribute ScreenConnect. </p><p>However, the latest campaign takes a different approach, using genuine, digitally signed software with attacker-controlled configurations rather than modifying the original binaries.</p><h3 id=\"sec-1\"><strong>Fake Pages and Screening Logic</strong></h3><p>The delivery pages present fake document-review prompts, including titles such as “View &amp; Sign Form” and “Sign in to view document.” When visitors access these pages, scripts check their operating systems and device types, redirecting visitors who are not using Windows desktop systems.</p><figure><a href=\"https://hackread.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Digitally-Signed-ScreenConnect-Installers-Used-in-Fake-Adobe-Acrobat-Campaign.png\"><img width=\"1024\" height=\"683\" src=\"/dailyinfo/media/c4ca8c3a36dd2d39.webp\" srcset=\"/dailyinfo/media/ec1dd20cd7e8a9c9.webp 720w, /dailyinfo/media/e0d0a5ca3dc9cf96.webp 1200w, /dailyinfo/media/c4ca8c3a36dd2d39.webp 1600w\" sizes=\"(min-width: 1536px) 760px, (min-width: 1024px) calc(100vw - 524px), (min-width: 640px) 608px, calc(100vw - 32px)\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" alt=\"\"></a><figcaption>Fake Adobe document-sharing page used in the campaign, with the browser’s download panel showing cancelled ScreenConnect installer downloads (Credit: CloudSEK)</figcaption></figure><p>The pages also collect public IP addresses, geolocation, browser, and ISP details before sending them to <a href=\"https://hackread.com/telegram-bots-stealing-one-time-passwords/\">Telegram bots</a>. They apply ISP-based restrictions that can prevent selected visitors from receiving the download. This filtering may help reduce exposure to automated security analysis, although CloudSEK could not establish its exact purpose.</p><p>Visitors who pass the checks automatically receive a ScreenConnect client. One Adobe-themed domain used in the campaign, <code>clickksy.icu</code>, was registered on October 2, 2026, and received a wildcard Let’s Encrypt certificate on the same day. Researchers could not directly retrieve the page because of a Cloudflare challenge.</p><h3 id=\"sec-2\"><strong>Configuration and Persistent Access</strong></h3><p>According to CloudSEK’s <a href=\"https://www.cloudsek.com/blog/fake-adobe-acrobat-lures-deliver-screenconnect-remote-access-clients\">report</a>, the threat relies on preconfigured settings inside the installers rather than modified software. The company identified two genuine, digitally signed installers associated with separate ScreenConnect tenants, omydze and vvvzou. </p><p>Follow-up collection identified three additional tenant configurations: <code>luminahealthturkey2, tfcconnection</code>, and <code>alunos1</code>. The signatures of those three additional clients were not independently verified.</p><p>The two Adobe-associated installers were configured to install ScreenConnect services that launch automatically at startup and run with LocalSystem privileges, according to CloudSEK’s static analysis. This could allow an external operator to maintain remote access to an infected computer.</p><p>Operators could then steal data, move across the network, or deploy extra software. CloudSEK noted that “valid vendor signatures do not make a remote-management client safe when it has been configured to connect to an unapproved tenant.”</p><figure><a href=\"https://hackread.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Digitally-Signed-ScreenConnect-Installers-Used-in-Fake-Adobe-Acrobat-Campaign-1.png\"><img width=\"1024\" height=\"563\" src=\"/dailyinfo/media/20a6da92d07eeff9.webp\" srcset=\"/dailyinfo/media/3e81dd9c94fd9c10.webp 720w, /dailyinfo/media/26c8a1c7bb410e4b.webp 1200w, /dailyinfo/media/20a6da92d07eeff9.webp 1600w\" sizes=\"(min-width: 1536px) 760px, (min-width: 1024px) calc(100vw - 524px), (min-width: 640px) 608px, calc(100vw - 32px)\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" alt=\"\"></a><figcaption>CloudSEK’s overview of the campaign, showing how fake document pages deliver ScreenConnect clients configured for attacker-controlled remote access. The diagram includes observed activity and inferred tactics (Credit: CloudSEK)</figcaption></figure><h3 id=\"sec-3\"><strong>Advice for Security Teams</strong></h3><p>CloudSEK has not confirmed any victim compromise or established who operates the infrastructure. The company advises organizations to look beyond file hashes and check their DNS and proxy logs for connections to unknown ScreenConnect servers.</p><p>Security teams should also check for unexpected ScreenConnect services on Windows machines and report suspicious tenant IDs to ConnectWise. Restricting ScreenConnect installations to approved tenants can also help prevent unauthorized remote access.</p>","zhKind","complete","后门","Adobe","Cloudflare","https://hackread.com/hackers-remote-access-tool-fake-adobe-acrobat-pages/","Hackread",{"_102":103,"_104":-5},"changelogVersion","2026-10-01T08:00","release"]
